<!-- ==================== SHEET 8 — CH.1 p.1 ==================== -->
<p class="chno">CHAPTER 1</p>
<p class="ch">Introduction</p>
<p class="h2">1.1&nbsp;&nbsp;Overview</p>
<p>The practice of software development has been reshaped over the last few years by large language
models. Tasks that previously demanded either an experienced colleague or a long search through
documentation &mdash; explaining an unfamiliar error, reviewing a function for correctness,
summarising a specification, drafting a first version of a module &mdash; can now be addressed
conversationally. The benefit, however, has arrived in a fragmented form. Each capability is
delivered by a different vendor, behind a different account, with a different interface and a
different privacy policy.</p>
<p><b>DevAssist AI</b> is a unified web platform that consolidates seven distinct developer
assistance capabilities behind one authenticated account and one coherent interface. It is aimed
primarily at engineering students and early-career developers, for whom the cost of assembling and
paying for a toolchain is a real barrier. The platform is deployed publicly and operates entirely on
free-tier cloud infrastructure, which makes the engineering of the system, and not merely its
feature list, a substantive part of the contribution.</p>
<p class="h2">1.2&nbsp;&nbsp;Motivation and Problem Statement</p>
<p>Three specific problems motivated this work. The first is <b>tool fragmentation</b>. A student
debugging an assignment may consult a chat assistant, paste the same code into a separate analysis
service, run it on a third online compiler, and practise the underlying concept on a fourth
competitive-programming site. No context is carried between them, and the same information is
re-entered repeatedly.</p>
<p>The second is <b>privacy of private material</b>. Asking questions about a personal document
normally means uploading it to a general-purpose service whose retention behaviour is opaque, and
whose answers may be influenced by material entirely outside that document.</p>
<p>The third is <b>cost</b>. Commercial assistants, managed vector databases and hosted execution
sandboxes are individually inexpensive but collectively out of reach for a student. The problem
addressed by this project is therefore to design and build a single, secure, multi-tenant platform
that delivers these capabilities together, grounds private queries strictly in user-owned data, and
sustains acceptable behaviour under the hard resource limits of free-tier hosting.</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 9 — CH.1 p.2 ==================== -->
<p class="h2" style="margin-top:0cm">1.3&nbsp;&nbsp;Objectives</p>
<p>The objectives of Phase I of this project are stated below.</p>
<ol>
<li>To design a three-tier, service-oriented architecture in which the presentation, business and
inference concerns are independently deployable and independently replaceable.</li>
<li>To implement seven functional modules &mdash; Global AI, Secret AI, Code Analyzer, Online
Compiler, VoidMain, Ignite and Circuit &mdash; behind a single authenticated session.</li>
<li>To enforce strict multi-tenant data isolation, so that every persisted record is bound to the
universally unique identifier of its owner and no endpoint can return another user's data.</li>
<li>To ground private document question-answering exclusively in material the user has uploaded, so
that no external corpus contributes to the answer.</li>
<li>To abstract large-language-model access behind a provider registry, so that the active vendor
and model can be changed through configuration alone, without modifying application code.</li>
<li>To design and evaluate mitigations that allow the platform to operate acceptably within
free-tier constraints on memory, request duration, request rate and token quota.</li>
<li>To validate the behaviour of the inference and orchestration layers through an automated test
suite in which all external model providers are mocked.</li>
</ol>
<p class="h2">1.4&nbsp;&nbsp;Scope of the Project</p>
<p>The scope of Phase I covers the complete design, implementation and public deployment of the
seven modules listed above, together with authentication, per-user persistence, request rate
limiting and token accounting. Code execution is delegated to an external sandboxing service, and
model inference is delegated to external providers; the project does not train or fine-tune any
model of its own.</p>
<p>The following are explicitly outside the scope of this phase: generation of backend or database
code by the Ignite module, which currently produces frontend projects only; a persistent vector
store, since document context is presently held in process memory; real-time collaborative editing;
and native mobile applications. These are treated as Phase II objectives and are set out in
Chapter 6.</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 10 — CH.1 p.3 ==================== -->
<p class="h2" style="margin-top:0cm">1.5&nbsp;&nbsp;Organization of the Report</p>
<p>The remainder of this report is organised as follows.</p>
<p><b>Chapter 2 &mdash; Literature Survey</b> reviews the classes of system that inform this work:
conversational programming assistants, retrieval-augmented generation, automated code review, remote
code execution sandboxes, online judging systems and program synthesis from natural language. Each
is examined for the specific technique it contributes and for the limitation it leaves unaddressed,
and the chapter closes with a comparative analysis and a statement of the research gap.</p>
<p><b>Chapter 3 &mdash; Aim and Scope of the Present Investigation</b> states the aim formally,
characterises the existing practice that the platform replaces, enumerates the limitations of that
practice, and describes the proposed system. It reports the technical, economic and operational
feasibility of the design and specifies the hardware, software, functional and non-functional
requirements.</p>
<p><b>Chapter 4 &mdash; System Design and Methodology</b> presents the architecture of the platform.
It describes the three deployed services and the contract between them, the responsibilities of each
functional module, the document grounding pipeline, the provider abstraction that decouples the
system from any single model vendor, the data flow diagrams at context and decomposition level, the
use case model and the database design that carries multi-tenant isolation.</p>
<p><b>Chapter 5 &mdash; Implementation and Results</b> describes the development and deployment
environment, the implementation of each module, the public REST interface, the testing strategy and
its outcome, and the qualitative and quantitative results observed from the deployed system,
illustrated with screenshots of the working interface.</p>
<p><b>Chapter 6 &mdash; Conclusion and Future Work</b> summarises what Phase I established, states
the limitations that remain open, and defines the objectives of Phase II.</p>
<p>The report closes with the list of references cited throughout.</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 11 — CH.2 p.4 ==================== -->
<p class="chno">CHAPTER 2</p>
<p class="ch">Literature Survey</p>
<p class="h2">2.1&nbsp;&nbsp;Introduction</p>
<p>DevAssist AI draws on six established lines of work: the transformer architecture and the
instruction-following models built upon it, retrieval-augmented generation, automated code analysis
and synthesis, sandboxed remote code execution, automated judging of competitive programming
submissions, and the architectural patterns used to compose these into a secure multi-tenant
service. This chapter reviews each line for the technique it contributes and for the limitation it
leaves open.</p>
<p class="h2">2.2&nbsp;&nbsp;Review of Existing Work</p>
<p><b>1. Transformer architecture.</b> Vaswani et al. [1] introduced the transformer, replacing
recurrence with self-attention and making it practical to train models on very large corpora. Every
model used by this platform descends from that architecture. The work is foundational rather than
applied: it establishes the capability but says nothing about how such a model should be exposed to
an end user, budgeted, or restricted to a private data set.</p>
<p><b>2. Few-shot instruction following.</b> Brown et al. [2] demonstrated that a sufficiently large
language model performs new tasks from a natural-language description and a handful of examples,
without gradient updates. This result is what allows a single deployed model in DevAssist AI to
serve seven functionally different modules through prompt construction alone. The limitation is
reliability: the same property that makes the model general also makes its output format unstable,
which the present work addresses by validating every structured response against a schema before it
reaches the user interface.</p>
<p><b>3. Code-trained models.</b> Chen et al. [3] evaluated models trained specifically on public
source code and reported both strong functional synthesis and a persistent rate of subtly incorrect
output. Their finding directly informs the design of the Code Analyzer module in this project, which
presents an optimised rewrite alongside the original in a difference view rather than substituting
it silently, leaving the developer as the final reviewer.</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 12 — CH.2 p.5 ==================== -->
<p style="margin-top:0cm"><b>4. Retrieval-augmented generation.</b> Lewis et al. [4] combined a
parametric generator with a non-parametric retriever, so that answers are conditioned on documents
fetched at inference time. This is the technique underlying the Secret AI module. Their formulation
assumes a retrieval index that can be held in memory; the present work departs from this assumption
because the deployment target cannot host an embedding model, and adopts a direct context-injection
strategy instead, as described in Section 4.3.</p>
<p><b>5. Dense passage retrieval.</b> Karpukhin et al. [5] showed that dense vector retrieval trained
with a dual encoder outperforms sparse lexical matching for open-domain question answering. Reimers
and Gurevych [6] made such encoders practical by producing sentence embeddings that are directly
comparable under cosine similarity. Together these define the retrieval quality that a persistent
vector store would give this platform, and they set the target for Phase II.</p>
<p><b>6. Efficient similarity search.</b> Johnson et al. [7] presented FAISS, a library for
billion-scale approximate nearest-neighbour search. A FAISS-backed vector store is implemented in
this project and is used in local execution, but it is not active in the deployed configuration
because loading the accompanying embedding model exceeds the memory ceiling of the free-tier
container. This trade-off is reported honestly in Section 4.3 rather than concealed.</p>
<p><b>7. Program synthesis from natural language.</b> Austin et al. [8] studied the generation of
complete programs from natural-language descriptions and observed that success degrades sharply as
the required output grows. The Ignite module responds to this finding structurally: rather than
requesting an entire project in one call, it first obtains a file-level plan and then generates each
file in a separate call, so that a failure is isolated to one file and can be retried
independently.</p>
<p><b>8. Remote execution sandboxes and online judges.</b> Public compilation services such as
Wandbox [15] and open-source judging systems such as Judge0 [14] establish the accepted pattern for
executing untrusted user code: isolate it in a disposable container outside the application process.
Both the Online Compiler and the VoidMain judge in this project follow this pattern by delegating
execution to an external sandbox, so that no user-supplied code ever runs on the application
servers.</p>
<p><b>9. Architecture and authentication.</b> Fielding [13] defined the REST constraints that govern
the interfaces between the three services here; Martin [11] and Newman [12] set out the layering and
service-decomposition rules the backend follows; and RFC 7519 [9] and RFC 6749 [10] define the token
and delegated-authorisation mechanisms used to carry identity from the browser to every protected
endpoint.</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 13 — CH.2 p.6 ==================== -->
<p class="h2" style="margin-top:0cm">2.3&nbsp;&nbsp;Comparative Analysis</p>
<table class="t">
<tr><th style="width:22%">System / Work</th><th style="width:30%">Principal Contribution</th><th style="width:26%">Limitation for this Use Case</th><th style="width:22%">Adoption in DevAssist AI</th></tr>
<tr><td>Transformer [1]</td><td>Self-attention; scalable sequence modelling</td><td>Architecture only; no delivery or governance model</td><td>Underlies every model invoked</td></tr>
<tr><td>Few-shot LLMs [2]</td><td>Task generalisation from instructions</td><td>Output format is not guaranteed</td><td>Schema validation on every structured reply</td></tr>
<tr><td>Code-trained LLMs [3]</td><td>Functional code synthesis</td><td>Silent subtle defects</td><td>Difference view; developer retains final review</td></tr>
<tr><td>RAG [4]</td><td>Answers conditioned on retrieved documents</td><td>Assumes an in-memory retrieval index</td><td>Context injection with per-document cache</td></tr>
<tr><td>DPR / SBERT [5][6]</td><td>High-quality dense retrieval</td><td>Embedding model exceeds free-tier memory</td><td>Implemented; deferred to Phase II</td></tr>
<tr><td>FAISS [7]</td><td>Efficient vector similarity search</td><td>Requires a resident index and model</td><td>Present in code; inactive in deployment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Program synthesis [8]</td><td>Whole-program generation</td><td>Quality falls with output length</td><td>Plan first, then one call per file</td></tr>
<tr><td>Judge0 / Wandbox [14][15]</td><td>Isolated execution of untrusted code</td><td>Availability of a public service is not guaranteed</td><td>External sandbox for compiler and judge</td></tr>
<tr><td>Commercial assistants</td><td>Mature conversational experience</td><td>Fragmented, paid, opaque data retention</td><td>Unified account; grounding restricted to user data</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="cap">Table 2.1 &ndash; Comparative analysis of reviewed work</p>
<p class="h2">2.4&nbsp;&nbsp;Research Gap and Inference</p>
<p>The review shows that each individual capability required by this project is well established in
isolation. Conversational assistance, grounded question answering, code analysis, sandboxed
execution, automated judging and program synthesis all have mature reference implementations. What
the literature does not address is their <b>integration under a single tenancy model</b>, and their
operation under <b>severe resource constraints</b>.</p>
<p>Two gaps follow. First, no reviewed system offers these capabilities together with a uniform
isolation guarantee binding a user's documents, conversations, submissions and generated projects to
one identity. Second, the reviewed retrieval techniques presuppose infrastructure &mdash; resident
embedding models, persistent indices, long-running processes &mdash; that free-tier hosting does not
provide, and the literature offers little guidance when that presupposition fails.</p>
<p>DevAssist AI addresses the first with a single identifier applied uniformly across every entity
in the schema, and the second with an explicit set of mitigations: keep-alive scheduling, lazy
importing, sliding-window rate limiting, daily token accounting and bounded context injection.</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 14 — CH.3 p.7 ==================== -->
<p class="chno">CHAPTER 3</p>
<p class="ch">Aim and Scope of the Present Investigation</p>
<p class="h2">3.1&nbsp;&nbsp;Aim of the Project</p>
<p>The aim of this project is to design, implement and publicly deploy a unified, secure,
multi-tenant developer assistance platform that delivers seven distinct AI-assisted capabilities
behind a single authenticated account, grounds all private document queries strictly in user-owned
material, decouples the system from any single model vendor through a configuration-level provider
abstraction, and sustains acceptable behaviour entirely within the resource limits of free-tier
cloud infrastructure.</p>
<p class="h2">3.2&nbsp;&nbsp;Existing System</p>
<p>The existing practice is not a single system but a habitual assembly of unrelated services: a
commercial assistant for general questions, a separate service for private documents, an editor
plugin or static analyser for code review, a public online compiler, a competitive-programming site
for practice, and a scaffolding generator for new projects.</p>
<p>Each authenticates the user separately, keeps its own copy of whatever is submitted, and imposes
its own conventions. Nothing is shared: a document uploaded to one is invisible to another, and a
conversation held in one cannot inform an analysis in the next.</p>
<p class="h2">3.3&nbsp;&nbsp;Limitations of the Existing System</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Context loss across tools.</b> Information is re-entered at every boundary, and reasoning
established in one tool is unavailable to the next.</li>
<li><b>Uncontrolled exposure of private material.</b> Documents go to services whose retention
policies are opaque, and answers may be shaped by material outside the document.</li>
<li><b>Cumulative cost.</b> Individually modest subscriptions become collectively prohibitive for a
student, and their free tiers are too restricted for sustained use.</li>
<li><b>Vendor lock-in.</b> Each service is bound to one provider, so an outage or price change
cannot be absorbed by the user.</li>
<li><b>No unified isolation guarantee.</b> With data scattered across providers there is no single,
auditable statement of who can access what.</li>
<li><b>Inconsistent interaction model.</b> Seven interfaces impose seven sets of conventions,
raising the learning cost.</li>
</ol>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 15 — CH.3 p.8 ==================== -->
<p class="h2" style="margin-top:0cm">3.4&nbsp;&nbsp;Proposed System</p>
<p>The proposed system is a single web platform, accessed through one authenticated session, that
exposes seven modules over a common design system and a common persistence layer. Its defining
properties are as follows.</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Unified tenancy.</b> Every persisted entity carries the universally unique identifier of its
owner and every read path filters on it, so isolation is a property of the schema rather than of
individual endpoint code.</li>
<li><b>Strict grounding for private data.</b> The model is supplied only with text extracted from
the document the user selected; no external corpus can contribute to the answer.</li>
<li><b>Provider independence.</b> Model access is resolved at request time through a registry keyed
by an environment variable. Changing vendor is a configuration change; adding one is a single class
and a single registry entry.</li>
<li><b>Delegated execution.</b> User-supplied code never runs on the application servers. It is
dispatched to an external sandbox and only its output is returned.</li>
<li><b>Explicit resource governance.</b> A per-user sliding-window rate limiter and a daily token
ledger bound consumption before any request reaches a quota-limited provider.</li>
<li><b>Free-tier operability.</b> A keep-alive scheduler suppresses cold starts, heavy libraries are
imported lazily, and generation is sequential with backoff so request duration stays within platform
timeouts.</li>
</ol>
<p class="h2">3.5&nbsp;&nbsp;Feasibility Study</p>
<p><b>Technical feasibility.</b> Every component rests on mature, documented technology: Spring Boot
for the service layer, FastAPI for inference orchestration, managed PostgreSQL for persistence and
vendor-hosted models over HTTP. No component required an unproven capability, and the complete
system has been deployed and exercised end to end.</p>
<p><b>Economic feasibility.</b> Recurring infrastructure cost is zero. Static hosting, both
application services, the database, authentication, storage and inference are all obtained on free
tiers, so the cost of the project is development effort alone.</p>
<p><b>Operational feasibility.</b> The interface follows conventions users already hold from
mainstream chat and editor applications, so adoption needs no training. Administration is limited to
environment configuration, and provider failure is recoverable by one variable change.</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 16 — CH.3 p.9 ==================== -->
