</table>
<p class="cap">Table 3.2 &ndash; Functional requirements</p>
<p class="h3">3.6.3&nbsp;&nbsp;Non-Functional Requirements</p>
<p><b>Security:</b> every route except the public landing and health checks requires a valid token,
and all credentials come from environment variables. <b>Isolation:</b> no response may contain data
owned by another identity. <b>Availability:</b> the inference service is kept warm on a
fourteen-minute schedule. <b>Performance:</b> responses stream progressively, so first output
appears well before completion. <b>Maintainability:</b> strict layer boundaries are observed, and a
provider change requires no code modification.</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 17 — CH.4 p.10 ==================== -->
<p class="chno">CHAPTER 4</p>
<p class="ch">System Design and Methodology</p>
<p class="h2">4.1&nbsp;&nbsp;System Architecture</p>
<p>DevAssist AI is organised as three independently deployed tiers. The <b>presentation tier</b> is a
set of static documents served from a content delivery network; it holds no secret and performs no
privileged operation. The <b>application tier</b> is a Spring Boot service that owns identity
verification, authorisation, persistence and rate limiting. The <b>inference tier</b> is a FastAPI
service that owns prompt construction, provider selection, response validation and token accounting.
Separating the last two matters: the Java service is stable and transactional, whereas the Python
service changes whenever a prompt or a model changes, and the two therefore have very different
release rhythms.</p>
<p>The browser never contacts the inference tier or any model vendor directly. Every request carries
a signed token, is verified by a servlet filter in the application tier, and is only then proxied
inward. This single ingress point is what makes the isolation guarantee auditable: there is exactly
one place where identity is established, and every downstream call inherits it.</p>
<p style="text-align:center"><img src="fig-4-1.svg" width="578" height="280" alt="[ Fig. 4.1 — insert from the PDF version ]"></p>
<p class="cap">Fig. 4.1 &ndash; Three-tier system architecture of DevAssist AI</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 18 — CH.4 p.11 ==================== -->
<p class="h2" style="margin-top:0cm">4.2&nbsp;&nbsp;Module Design</p>
<p>The platform is decomposed into seven functional modules. Each owns its own routes, its own
entities and its own prompt strategy, but all share the same authentication filter, the same
persistence layer and the same provider abstraction. Table 4.1 summarises the responsibility and the
principal design decision of each.</p>
<table class="t">
<tr><th style="width:16%">Module</th><th style="width:40%">Responsibility</th><th style="width:44%">Principal Design Decision</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Global AI</b></td><td>General-purpose conversational assistance with persistent, searchable, pinnable threads.</td><td>Responses are streamed token by token so that first output appears early; history is stored relationally as threads and messages rather than as opaque blobs, which makes search and per-thread deletion straightforward.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Secret AI</b></td><td>Question answering restricted to documents the user has uploaded.</td><td>Extracted text is bounded and cached per document under a lock; the model receives only that text, so no external corpus can influence the answer.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Code Analyzer</b></td><td>Structured review of submitted source code.</td><td>A deterministic pattern pre-pass produces optimisation hints that are appended to the prompt, so the model is guided rather than trusted blindly; the rewrite is shown in a difference view, never applied silently.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Online Compiler</b></td><td>Multi-language edit, compile and execute with standard input support.</td><td>Execution is delegated to an external sandbox; no user code runs in the application process.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>VoidMain</b></td><td>Competitive-programming judge with problem set, verdicts, history and leaderboard.</td><td>Submissions are executed through the same sandbox and compared against expected output; verdicts and rankings are persisted per identity.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Ignite</b></td><td>Generation of a complete multi-file frontend project from one prompt.</td><td>A planning call returns a file list, after which each file is generated in its own streamed call; failure is isolated to a single file and retried with backoff.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Circuit</b></td><td>Step-by-step animated visual explanation of a concept or algorithm.</td><td>The model returns strict JSON only, validated against a schema with one corrective retry; the interface, not the model, renders the animation.</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="cap">Table 4.1 &ndash; Module responsibilities and design decisions</p>
<p>Two cross-cutting services support all seven. <b>RateLimitService</b> maintains an in-memory
sliding window per identity and rejects requests that exceed the configured hourly allowance before
any provider is contacted. <b>KeepAliveService</b> issues a scheduled request to the inference tier
every fourteen minutes, which keeps the container resident and removes the multi-second cold start
that would otherwise precede the first request after a period of inactivity.</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 19 — CH.4 p.12 ==================== -->
<p class="h2" style="margin-top:0cm">4.3&nbsp;&nbsp;Document Grounding Pipeline</p>
<p>The Secret AI module answers questions about a document without consulting anything outside it.
The canonical retrieval-augmented design [4] would embed the document into chunks, index them, and
retrieve the nearest neighbours of the question. That design is implemented in this project, but it
is <i>not</i> the deployed configuration: loading a sentence-embedding model together with a vector
index exceeds the memory ceiling of the free-tier container, and the resulting cold start exceeded
the platform request timeout. The deployed pipeline therefore substitutes bounded context injection
for retrieval, as shown in Fig. 4.2.</p>
<p>Extracted text is truncated to a fixed character budget and held in a per-document cache guarded
by a lock, so that concurrent questions about the same document neither re-extract it nor corrupt
the shared map. Conversation history is keyed separately by session, which allows several
independent discussions of the same document.</p>
<p style="text-align:center"><img src="fig-4-2.svg" width="578" height="142" alt="[ Fig. 4.2 — insert from the PDF version ]"></p>
<p class="cap">Fig. 4.2 &ndash; Document grounding pipeline of the Secret AI module</p>
<p class="h2">4.4&nbsp;&nbsp;Provider Abstraction and Failover</p>
<p>All model access passes through a registry of provider classes that satisfy a common generation
contract. The active provider is resolved per request from an environment variable, and each
provider reads its own model identifier from its own variable. Switching vendor is therefore a
configuration change with no code modification, and adding a vendor is one class plus one registry
entry. Every provider implements three retries with exponential backoff and distinguishes a
rate-limit condition, which is retryable, from a client error such as an invalid key, which is
not.</p>
<p>This abstraction was validated in practice. When the primary provider's inference queue backed up
and requests began exceeding the read timeout, service was restored by changing a single variable to
the alternate provider. No application code was modified and no logic was redeployed.</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 20 — CH.4 p.13 ==================== -->
