<!-- ==================== SHEET 21 — CH.4 p.14 ==================== -->
<p class="h2" style="margin-top:0cm">4.6&nbsp;&nbsp;Use Case Model</p>
<p>A single primary actor, the authenticated user, drives all eight use cases. Two supporting actors
are external systems that the platform invokes rather than serves.</p>
<p style="text-align:center"><img src="fig-4-5.svg" width="578" height="220" alt="[ Fig. 4.5 — insert from the PDF version ]"></p>
<p class="cap">Fig. 4.5 &ndash; Use case diagram of the platform</p>
<p class="h2">4.7&nbsp;&nbsp;Database Design and Isolation</p>
<p>The schema is deliberately simple. A single <b>users</b> table carries the identity issued by the
authentication provider, and every other table stores that same universally unique identifier as a
foreign key. Because isolation is expressed once, in the shape of the data, no endpoint can
accidentally omit it without the omission being visible in the query itself.</p>
<p style="text-align:center"><img src="fig-4-6.svg" width="578" height="157" alt="[ Fig. 4.6 — insert from the PDF version ]"></p>
<p class="cap">Fig. 4.6 &ndash; Entity relationship diagram of the persistence layer</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 22 — CH.5 p.15 ==================== -->
<p class="chno">CHAPTER 5</p>
<p class="ch">Implementation and Results</p>
<p class="h2">5.1&nbsp;&nbsp;Development and Deployment Environment</p>
<table class="t">
<tr><th style="width:24%">Layer</th><th style="width:38%">Technology</th><th style="width:38%">Deployment</th></tr>
<tr><td>Frontend</td><td>HTML5, CSS3, vanilla JavaScript, Monaco editor</td><td>Static hosting on a global CDN</td></tr>
<tr><td>Backend</td><td>Spring Boot 3, Java 21, Maven, Spring Data JPA</td><td>Docker image on a managed container host</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inference</td><td>FastAPI, Python 3.11, Uvicorn, Pydantic</td><td>Managed Python runtime</td></tr>
<tr><td>Persistence</td><td>PostgreSQL 15 behind a transaction pooler</td><td>Managed database service</td></tr>
<tr><td>Identity</td><td>Managed authentication with signed tokens</td><td>Managed authentication service</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inference vendor</td><td>Groq (primary), NVIDIA NIM (registered alternate)</td><td>Vendor-hosted HTTPS endpoints</td></tr>
<tr><td>Execution</td><td>External compilation and execution sandbox</td><td>Public sandbox service</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="cap">Table 5.1 &ndash; Development and deployment environment</p>
<p class="h2">5.2&nbsp;&nbsp;Module Implementation</p>
<p>The backend observes strict layer boundaries. Controllers validate input and return a response
entity; they hold no business logic and make no repository call. Services hold the business logic
and own transaction boundaries. Repositories are Spring Data interfaces, so no structured query
language appears in the service layer. Dependencies are supplied through constructors rather than
field injection, which keeps them explicit and makes every service testable with mocked
collaborators. Data transfer objects are Java records.</p>
<p>In the inference tier, all model access is funnelled through a single generation wrapper, so that
budget checking and usage recording apply uniformly and cannot be bypassed by a new route. Shared
in-process state &mdash; the document cache and the two conversation history maps &mdash; is
accessed only under its corresponding lock, because the server handles concurrent requests in a
thread pool. Heavy scientific libraries are imported inside the function that needs them rather than
at module scope, which keeps the cold-start time of the container within the platform's request
timeout.</p>
<p>Two implementation details are worth recording. First, the Ignite module never asks for an entire
project in one request: a planning call returns a strict file list, and each file is then produced in
its own streamed call, so a rate-limit rejection costs one file rather than the whole project.
Second, the Circuit module accepts only strict JSON from the model, validates it against a schema,
and permits exactly one corrective retry before returning a friendly error &mdash; the browser,
never the model, renders the resulting animation.</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 23 — CH.5 p.16 ==================== -->
<p class="h2" style="margin-top:0cm">5.3&nbsp;&nbsp;REST Interface Summary</p>
<table class="t">
<tr><th style="width:34%">Endpoint</th><th style="width:14%">Method</th><th style="width:52%">Purpose and isolation basis</th></tr>
<tr><td>/api/auth/sync</td><td class="c">POST</td><td>Register or refresh the local identity record from the verified token</td></tr>
<tr><td>/ai/upload/{userId}</td><td class="c">POST</td><td>Store a document against the owning identity</td></tr>
<tr><td>/ai/documents/{userId}</td><td class="c">GET</td><td>List only the documents owned by that identity</td></tr>
<tr><td>/ai/rag</td><td class="c">POST</td><td>Answer a question grounded in one owned document</td></tr>
<tr><td>/api/global-chat/all</td><td class="c">GET</td><td>List conversation threads filtered by owner</td></tr>
<tr><td>/api/global-chat/{chatId}/message</td><td class="c">POST</td><td>Append a message after verifying thread ownership</td></tr>
<tr><td>/ai/analyze</td><td class="c">POST</td><td>Return structured analysis of submitted source code</td></tr>
<tr><td>/vm/submit</td><td class="c">POST</td><td>Judge a submission and record the verdict per identity</td></tr>
<tr><td>/api/project-builder/plan</td><td class="c">POST</td><td>Return a file-level plan for a described project</td></tr>
<tr><td>/api/project-builder/generate-file</td><td class="c">POST</td><td>Stream the contents of one planned file</td></tr>
<tr><td>/api/circuit/ask</td><td class="c">POST</td><td>Return a schema-validated visual explanation</td></tr>
<tr><td>/api/usage</td><td class="c">GET</td><td>Report the caller's tokens against the daily budget</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="cap">Table 5.2 &ndash; Representative REST endpoints (all require a valid bearer token)</p>
<p class="h2">5.4&nbsp;&nbsp;Testing</p>
<p>Automated tests cover both application tiers. No test contacts a live model provider; every
provider is replaced by a stub, so the suite is deterministic and free of quota consumption.</p>
<table class="t">
<tr><th style="width:26%">Area</th><th style="width:22%">Framework</th><th style="width:52%">What is verified</th></tr>
<tr><td>Provider registry</td><td>pytest</td><td>Resolution by variable, retry and backoff, rate-limit versus client-error classification</td></tr>
<tr><td>Project builder</td><td>pytest</td><td>Plan parsing, fence stripping, path safety, context budgeting</td></tr>
<tr><td>Token ledger and quota</td><td>pytest</td><td>Usage recording, budget rejection, quota response shape</td></tr>
<tr><td>Circuit specification</td><td>pytest</td><td>Schema validation, corrective retry, malformed replies</td></tr>
<tr><td>Controllers</td><td>JUnit 5, MockMvc</td><td>Status codes, payload validation, token propagation, streaming</td></tr>
<tr><td>Services</td><td>JUnit 5, Mockito</td><td>Business rules and ownership checks with mocked repositories</td></tr>
<tr><td>Security</td><td>JUnit 5</td><td>Token parsing and rejection of unauthenticated requests</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rate limiting</td><td>JUnit 5</td><td>Sliding-window admission at the configured allowance</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="cap">Table 5.3 &ndash; Test coverage by area</p>
<p>The Python suite comprises six test modules with a shared fixture module; the Java suite
comprises sixteen test classes spanning controllers, services, security and configuration.</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 24 — CH.5 p.17 ==================== -->
<p class="h2" style="margin-top:0cm">5.5&nbsp;&nbsp;Results and Discussion</p>
<p>All seven modules are implemented and operate against the publicly deployed system. Fig. 5.1 to
Fig. 5.4 show the working interface of four representative modules.</p>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse">
<tr>
<td class="shot" style="width:49%"><p>Fig. 5.1 &mdash; insert screenshot:<br>Global AI conversational interface<br>(streamed answer, thread list, code block)</p></td>
<td style="border:none;width:2%"></td>
<td class="shot" style="width:49%"><p>Fig. 5.2 &mdash; insert screenshot:<br>Secret AI document library<br>and a grounded answer</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:none"><p class="cap">Fig. 5.1 &ndash; Global AI conversational interface</p></td>
<td style="border:none"></td>
<td style="border:none"><p class="cap">Fig. 5.2 &ndash; Secret AI document library and grounded answer</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class="shot"><p>Fig. 5.3 &mdash; insert screenshot:<br>Code Analyzer panels with<br>difference view and complexity chart</p></td>
<td style="border:none"></td>
<td class="shot"><p>Fig. 5.4 &mdash; insert screenshot:<br>Ignite generating a project with<br>live file tree and progress</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="border:none"><p class="cap">Fig. 5.3 &ndash; Code Analyzer output and complexity chart</p></td>
<td style="border:none"></td>
<td style="border:none"><p class="cap">Fig. 5.4 &ndash; Ignite project generation with live file tree</p></td>
</tr>
</table>
<p>Three results are worth discussing. <b>Isolation held under test.</b> With two accounts exercised
in parallel, no listing endpoint returned a record belonging to the other identity, which supports
expressing isolation in the schema rather than in endpoint code. <b>Provider abstraction proved its
value in production.</b> When the primary vendor's queue backed up and read timeouts appeared,
service was restored by changing one environment variable; no code was altered. <b>The free-tier
mitigations are necessary, not decorative.</b> Without keep-alive scheduling the first request after
an idle period incurred a multi-second cold start; without lazy importing the container exceeded its
memory ceiling at startup; and without per-file generation, a single rate-limit rejection failed the
whole project.</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

<!-- ==================== SHEET 25 — CH.6 p.18 ==================== -->
<p class="chno">CHAPTER 6</p>
<p class="ch">Conclusion and Future Work</p>
<p class="h2">6.1&nbsp;&nbsp;Conclusion</p>
<p>Phase I of this project has delivered a working, publicly deployed platform that consolidates
seven AI-assisted developer capabilities behind a single authenticated account. The three-tier
design separates a static presentation layer, a transactional Java application layer and a Python
inference layer, so that the component with the fastest rate of change &mdash; prompts and model
selection &mdash; can be revised without disturbing the components that hold user data.</p>
<p>Two design decisions proved more valuable than anticipated. Expressing multi-tenant isolation in
the schema, by carrying the owner's identifier on every entity, made the guarantee uniform across
all seven modules and removed the possibility of an endpoint quietly omitting it. Routing every
model call through a provider registry resolved at request time allowed a real vendor degradation to
be absorbed by a single configuration change, with no code modification and no redeployment of
application logic.</p>
<p>The project also demonstrates that the obstacles to running a multi-service platform on free
infrastructure are addressable by design rather than by expenditure. Cold starts, memory ceilings,
request timeouts and vendor rate limits were each met with a specific, testable mitigation.</p>
<p class="h2">6.2&nbsp;&nbsp;Limitations</p>
<p>Four limitations are acknowledged. Document context is held in process memory and is lost when
the inference container restarts, which on a free tier is frequent. The vector store is implemented
but inactive, so retrieval quality is bounded by a fixed context budget rather than by semantic
relevance. Ignite generates frontend projects only. Finally, the results in Chapter 5 are
observational; no controlled benchmark has yet been run.</p>
<p class="h2">6.3&nbsp;&nbsp;Future Work</p>
<p>Phase II will address these in order. A persistent vector store, using the vector extension of
the existing database, will replace bounded context injection and make retrieval quality measurable.
Ignite will be extended beyond the browser to server and schema code. Quota exhaustion, currently a
silent failure, will be surfaced as an explicit message with a retry time. The interface will be
made responsive for small screens, the problem set expanded and a contest mode added. Finally, a
formal evaluation will measure latency per provider, retrieval accuracy on a labelled document set,
generation time against project size, and token consumption per module.</p>

<br clear="all" class="pgbreak">

